Haah's three-dimensional cubic code has no string-like logical
  operators and possesses a logarithmic energy barrier.  Under Davies
  dynamics, Bravyi and Haah's Theorem~1 gives, for lattice sizes encoding
  two qubits and every fixed $0<a<1$, a lifetime bound of the form
  \begin{equation}
    T_{\mathrm{mem}}\ge C_{\varepsilon}L^{ac\beta-3}
    \qquad\text{when}\qquad
    L\le e^{(1-a)\beta/3},
    \label{eq:p9-cubic-code-lifetime}
  \end{equation}
  where $c>0$ and $C_{\varepsilon}>0$ are independent of $L$ and $\beta$.
  The bound in Eq.~\eqref{eq:p9-cubic-code-lifetime} establishes partial
  self-correction at low temperature.  Its admissible size is bounded at
  fixed $\beta$, so this bound does not establish the thermodynamic
  divergence in Eq.~\eqref{eq:p9-self-correction-condition}
  \sourcecite{ref:p9-bravyi-haah}{BH13}.
